==Republican Period (2029 to 2073)==

At the Liberal Party of Canada convention in January 2012, its members debated on a motion to include severing ties with the monarchy as a party platform. The proposed motion was rejected by 67%. However, the debate was held again in 2027 in the reign of King Charles III, and the motion was passed by a meagre 53%. Following increasingly onset problems as a result from the [[The Flood|Flood]], an emergency government was declared and the plans for full separation from the crown was set in motion after 3 July, 2029. This was met by widespread protests and fierce resistance from Conservatives.

The country would see itself weaken politically as a result of the resurgent nationalism which gripped the world at the time, culminating in Canada dropping the monarchy in favor of a new Constitution in 2029 that turned the country into a Republic. It also resulted in the secession of Quebec and the First Nations in 2028 and 2048 respectively. The office outlined in the Constitution of 2029, established a president elected by the House of Commons as both head of state and head of government, modeled off of that in South Africa. The Deputy President was also empowered by this new constitution, having authority in the Canadian Parliament.

John Edwick was the last President of Canada, having served from 24 October, 2070 until the Union Act of 2073 (10 January, 2073), which formally annexed Canada into the United States along with [[British Republic|Britain]], New Zealand, and [[Australia]].

